# ── dist-kernel ────────────────────────────────────────────────
# `grub` was here on the strength of a comment claiming grub was "still what
# boots". It was not. efibootmgr:
#
#   BootCurrent: 0005
#   BootOrder:   0005,0008,...
#   Boot0005* Limine   \EFI\Limine\BOOTX64.EFI
#   Boot0008* Gentoo   \EFI\Gentoo\grubx64.efi
#
# Limine is current and first; grub was second with a grub.cfg last written
# 2026-07-20 and never regenerated since. The flag was not inert either — it
# installs /usr/lib/kernel/postinst.d/91-grub-mkconfig.install, which ran on
# every kernel update to rebuild the config of a bootloader nothing used, and
# it is what made sys-kernel/installkernel depend on sys-boot/grub at all.
#
# dracut stays: it builds the initramfs that 95-limine.install then mirrors to
# the ESP.
sys-kernel/installkernel dracut

# ── OBS ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The base profile builds obs-studio with USE="alsa" and nothing else, which
# on this machine means a capture tool that cannot capture. wlroots exposes
# no X11 screen to grab and offers no compositor-specific capture API to OBS;
# the only route is the xdg-desktop-portal ScreenCast interface, and OBS only
# builds that path under USE=pipewire. Without it you get an OBS whose only
# working source is a webcam.
#   wayland    — native Wayland client (else it runs through XWayland)
#   pipewire   — portal screen capture AND PipeWire audio, the one that matters
#   pulseaudio — pipewire-pulse is what's running, so this is the audio path
#   v4l        — the Surface's built-in camera as a video source
# Deliberately NOT enabled: browser (pulls a ~200 MB prebuilt CEF for browser
# sources, which is a streaming-overlay feature, not a recording one).
media-video/obs-studio wayland pipewire pulseaudio v4l
# OBS hard-depends on ffmpeg[opus,x264] — that pair IS the recording output
# (H.264 video + Opus audio), not an optional codec nicety, so obs-studio
# simply will not resolve without them. The base profile ships ffmpeg with
# both off, so this is a rebuild of ffmpeg, not a flag flip on a fresh one.
media-video/ffmpeg opus x264

# ── fonts ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# nerdfonts defaults to symbols-only. Every config in this repo asks for
# "JetBrainsMono Nerd Font" by name, so it has to be enabled explicitly or
# waybar/ghostty/foot/rofi all silently fall back to a default face.
media-fonts/nerdfonts jetbrainsmono

# ── Qt (for OBS) ───────────────────────────────────────────────
# obs-studio 32.x's frontend reaches for QNativeInterface::QWaylandApplication
# to talk to the compositor directly. That symbol only exists in a qtbase built
# with USE=wayland; without it the build dies in OBSApp.cpp with
# "'QWaylandApplication' is not a member of 'QNativeInterface'" — a compile
# error rather than a dependency resolution failure, which is why portage
# happily starts the build and then fails 448 targets in.
#
# The base profile ships qtbase with -wayland, so this is a rebuild of qtbase
# (and it pulls dev-qt/qtwayland), not a flag flip on a fresh package. That is
# the real cost of OBS on this machine, and it is why the apps phase is
# optional.
#
# opengl is not optional alongside it: qtbase declares REQUIRED_USE
# "wayland? ( opengl )", so wayland on its own fails dependency resolution
# outright rather than building something degraded. The base profile has
# -opengl, so both flags are needed together.
dev-qt/qtbase wayland opengl

# ── absorbed from undeclared /etc/portage files (2026-07-26) ───
# These were live on the machine but in files this repo did not own, so a
# rebuild from a clean checkout would have silently dropped them. Two came from
# portage's own `--autounmask-write` (package.use/zz-autounmask), the rest from
# earlier hand setup (package.use/{system,webkit-gtk}).
#
# Most of what portage's autounmask wrote for the swaync/gtk4 chain was already
# declared above (gtk4-layer-shell verbatim, and gui-libs/gtk + mesa
# unversioned, which is broader than the versioned copies it generated).
#
# CORRECTION (2026-07-26, second pass): an earlier version of this comment
# claimed *everything* in zz-autounmask was already covered. Two entries were
# not, and both are load-bearing. They were found by checking the file entry by
# entry instead of trusting the claim, before deleting it:
#
#   x11-libs/gtk+[wayland]        required by gui-apps/waybar — the bar itself
#   dev-libs/libdbusmenu[gtk3]    required by xfce4-panel[dbusmenu], which
#                                 thunar[trash-panel-plugin] pulls in
#
# Both flags are active on the installed packages right now, so deleting
# zz-autounmask without these lines would not have broken the running system —
# it would have broken the next rebuild of waybar or thunar, at which point the
# cause would have been three weeks in the past.
x11-libs/gtk+ wayland
dev-libs/libdbusmenu gtk3

